1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Welcome to the first lesson. Workflow and agent are very different things. One runs on fixed code

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
paths that you write. The other lets the model decide its next move at runtime. Knowing which

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
one a job needs is the first design call that you'll make. Let's dive in. So what's the actual

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,000
line between a workflow and an agent? In a workflow, you write the control flow. The steps run

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
in a fixed order you laid out in code, and the model just fills in each step. In an agent,

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
the model itself decides what to do next. It reasons, calls a tool, looks at the result,

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
and chooses the following action in a loop until the job is done. The tell isn't whether tools get

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
used. Both use tools. The real question is who owns the next decision. If your code owns it,

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:57,000
that's a workflow. If the model owns it at runtime, that's an agent. Before you reach for

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
either one, know the building block they share. Anthropic calls it the augmented language model,

11
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
and it's just the model enhanced with a few capabilities. The first is retrieval, pulling

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
in relevant context so the model answers from real information instead of guessing. The second

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
is tools, letting the model take actions like querying a database or calling a service. The

14
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:28,000
third is memory, so it can carry state across a task. Most of the time, a single call with good

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
retrieval and a couple of examples already solves the problem. Start there. You build agents and

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
workflows on top of this block, not instead of it. Here's the mental model I want you to keep.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Every agentic system sits somewhere on one axis, and that axis is control. On one end,

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
your code decides the order of operations. You can read the whole path in your source,

19
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
top to bottom. On the other end, the model decides the order at runtime based on what it sees as it

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:06,000
goes. That single difference drives everything else. A fixed path is predictable and easy to test

21
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
because it does the same thing every time. A model-driven path is flexible because it can

22
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
adapt to inputs you didn't anticipate. When you sketch a design, ask where on that line it should

23
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:24,000
sit and why. Anthropic's guidance here is refreshingly blunt. Find the simplest solution

24
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,000
that works and only add complexity when it clearly improves the outcome. That matters because agents

25
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,000
are the expensive option. Every extra turn spends tokens and adds latency, so you don't

26
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,000
want that machinery unless the task genuinely needs it. A surprising number of real problems

27
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
are solved by one well-crafted model, call with retrieval, and a couple of examples. No loop,

28
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
no orchestration. So resist the urge to build the impressive thing first. Start with the plainest

29
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:01,000
design that could work, measure it, and only climb the complexity ladder when the results demand it.

30
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Simple is a feature, not a compromise. So when is an agent actually the right call? Three signals

31
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:14,000
point that way, and you usually want more than one before you commit. The first is an open-ended

32
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:21,000
space where the task can unfold in many directions you can't enumerate up front. The second is

33
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,000
unknown steps where you genuinely can't predict how many actions the job will take. The third is

34
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
when the model chooses tools, when part of the value is letting it pick which tool to use and

35
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:39,000
when. If a request has a bounded knowable path, none of these fire and a workflow will serve you

36
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
better. When two or three light up together, that's your cue to build an agent. When a feature request

37
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,000
lands on your desk, run the cheap test first. Ask whether a deterministic workflow could handle it

38
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:58,000
before you reach for a loop. Can you write the steps out in order? Does the same input always

39
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
take the same path? If yes, a workflow is almost certainly enough, and you'll get predictability

40
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,000
for free. There are three classic shapes to reach for here, and we'll name them next. The point is

41
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,000
that a lot of work that feels agent-shaped is really just a fixed sequence of model calls

42
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
with some glue code. Prove the workflow can't do the job before you pay for an agent.

43
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:29,000
That order saves you cost and headaches. Three workflow shapes cover most deterministic designs,

44
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
and knowing them keeps you from overbuilding. Prompt chaining breaks a task into a fixed

45
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
sequence where each model call works on the previous output. You can even add a simple

46
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:47,000
check between steps to catch problems early. Routing classifies an incoming request and sends

47
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:53,000
it to the handler built for that case, like sorting support tickets by type. Parallelization

48
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
runs independent pieces at the same time, either splitting a job into sections or running the same

49
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:06,000
task a few times and comparing results. In all three, you defined the structure. The model fills

50
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
in the reasoning, but your code still owns the path, which is exactly what makes them predictable.

51
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Let's put the decision into one clean habit. Start by writing the task down as a sequence

52
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
of steps. If you can, and the path is stable, build the workflow and stop there. If you find

53
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
yourself unable to enumerate the steps or the request keeps branching in ways you can't script,

54
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
that's when an agent earns its keep, and you don't have to pick one for the whole system.

55
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:41,000
A common, powerful design wraps a small agent inside a single workflow stage, so the deterministic

56
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,000
parts stay deterministic, and the model only drives where real flexibility is needed. Match

57
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,000
the design to the task in front of you, not to the architecture you wish you were building.

58
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Let's recap. A workflow runs on fixed paths you write in the code. An agent lets the model choose

59
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
the next step at runtime. Keep in mind that using tools does not make something an agent,

60
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:13,000
it's about who owns the next decision. And that covers when to build an agent versus a workflow.

61
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,000
I'll see you in the next lesson.

